  "report": "The CSV contains 8000 rows and 30 columns. There is strong, reproducible one-dimensional nonlinear structure: a noisy affine image of the (2,3) torus trefoil knot. The three map expressions recover it. Each is a five-column integer sum reduced modulo 1; the three column groups are disjoint. The recovered curve lies strictly inside the unit cube, so it never crosses a wrap border. Its observed coordinate ranges are approximately [[0.4095, 0.6388], [0.2939, 0.736], [0.3149, 0.7726]]. More explicitly, for 0<=t<2*pi define q(t)=((2+cos(3*t))*cos(2*t),(2+cos(3*t))*sin(2*t),sin(3*t)). The fitted curve is c+q(t)*M, with row-vector convention, c=[0.52739255, 0.52697853, 0.52848863] and M=[[-0.0029776, 0.01642246, 0.07591843], [0.0149736, -0.06931205, 0.01782281], [0.07730013, 0.01408416, -0.00066374]]. The drawing samples this closed curve at 200 equally spaced values of t and joins consecutive points, including the last to the first. No surface faces are warranted. Typical perpendicular scatter is consistent with approximately 0.005 noise per recovered coordinate. Method: inspected only X.csv; checked marginal distributions, correlations, PCA, pairwise mutual information, nearest-neighbor geometry, and real-versus-column-shuffled classifiers. Those basic checks looked like noise. Searched sparse integer Fourier characters using products of one-, two-, and three-column complex exponentials. The decisive search allowed five participating columns with coefficients +/-1 or +/-2. The three winning Fourier magnitudes were 0.934, 0.759, 0.731 on 2000 discovery rows and 0.930, 0.757, 0.724 on a disjoint 4000-row test set. A local-neighbor spectral embedding ordered the recovered loop. A standard affine trefoil fitted on 4000 training rows matched held-out rows as accurately as a flexible Fourier principal curve, supporting a genuine simple knot rather than sample-specific wiggles. For the 200-segment training-only drawing, held-out Euclidean RMS distance was 0.007082 (or 0.02453 after division by uniform-noise standard deviation 1/sqrt(12)). Median held-out distance was 0.005894, versus 0.326351 for 200000 independent uniform-noise outputs. At the training 99th-percentile distance cutoff 0.015005, 98.83% of held-out rows were accepted, compared with 0.176% of uniform noise and 0.200% of independently column-shuffled CSV rows. Because the map uses disjoint column groups and each contains a coefficient 1, independent Uniform(0,1) input columns produce exactly independent Uniform(0,1) outputs. The final drawing refits only the validated affine trefoil to all rows. Full analysis steps are logged in analysis.log; scripts and plots are saved alongside the submission. No other data files or internet sources were used."
